1
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:18,586
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems to be, well, it's a nice practice, somehow easier.

2
00:00:20,267 --> 00:00:28,676
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not so confusing, like, just watching the state of your mind, and then just being honest.

3
00:00:30,392 --> 00:00:34,716
[SPEAKER_00]: By seeing the state of your mind, you don't lose the perspective.

4
00:00:34,796 --> 00:00:35,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, where does this go?

5
00:00:36,877 --> 00:00:39,259
[SPEAKER_00]: When you don't see the state of your mind, why do you go with it?

6
00:00:55,524 --> 00:01:15,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you know it might end in... that will most likely... well, more certainly end in pain, and displeasure, and disappointment, or whatever, you kind of go ahead with it, because you don't... you haven't maintained a perspective to seeing that it's your general state of mind that was directing you there, not that particular thing.

7
00:01:16,687 --> 00:01:20,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it is relatively easy if the person is honest.

8
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:28,534
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, you know, different people can find different things easier in terms of practice.

9
00:01:28,654 --> 00:01:29,774
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, like, it's not...

10
00:01:30,535 --> 00:01:36,118
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, as we spoke yesterday, it's not just, oh, just watch your mind.

11
00:01:36,738 --> 00:01:37,839
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't really make sense.

12
00:01:37,959 --> 00:01:41,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it makes sense, but it's so general that it doesn't really make a difference.

13
00:01:41,541 --> 00:01:42,321
[SPEAKER_01]: So, watch your mind.

14
00:01:42,341 --> 00:01:43,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what can you mind?

15
00:01:44,811 --> 00:01:53,093
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the... when we spoke yesterday, like, when we said watching the state of your mind means watching the background of your mind, which is what citta is, really.

16
00:01:54,053 --> 00:02:05,596
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that peripheral, the general, like, mood, cloud, whatever we called it in the past, that kind of just like a general sort of leaning towards certain directions.

17
00:02:05,616 --> 00:02:08,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's how you can see the mind in terms of the five aggregates.

18
00:02:09,377 --> 00:02:12,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, as we spoke, kind of the most obvious defilements.

19
00:02:12,718 --> 00:02:14,418
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, oh, like, am I not

20
00:02:15,037 --> 00:02:26,044
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have any particular sensual desire right here, right now, in front of me, exciting or disturbing me, but the background of my mind is sort of affected by that.

21
00:02:26,064 --> 00:02:33,328
[SPEAKER_01]: There's like a cloud of sensuality, big, heavy in the background, nothing particularly manifested, but it's there, kind of directing me.

22
00:02:33,648 --> 00:02:36,670
[SPEAKER_01]: And if people are not mindful of that, they'll end up

23
00:02:37,096 --> 00:02:48,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, particularizing that very, like, general cloud of sensuality or ill-will, like, they just feel in a bad mood, so they take it out on somebody eventually, if they're very unrestrained.

24
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:55,063
[SPEAKER_01]: If they're a bit more restrained, they won't take it on anybody else, but then they've just been in an ill-will-like mood internally.

25
00:02:56,423 --> 00:03:00,045
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all coming from the general, kind of, peripheral, sort of,

26
00:03:01,072 --> 00:03:03,134
[SPEAKER_01]: background of your particular experience.

27
00:03:03,234 --> 00:03:04,354
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the mind is.

28
00:03:04,475 --> 00:03:05,215
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you never...

29
00:03:05,455 --> 00:03:06,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just watch your mind.

30
00:03:06,636 --> 00:03:13,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, people is all just looking at what's right in front of them, which means they directly don't see what their mind is, because the mind is not there in front.

31
00:03:13,261 --> 00:03:14,582
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is the background of that.

32
00:03:15,743 --> 00:03:20,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the moods, all of those are, like, kind of a manifestation of that mind.

33
00:03:21,347 --> 00:03:28,072
[SPEAKER_01]: But if somebody keeps an eye on the general, sort of, cloud of one's mind, that ambiguous, sort of,

34
00:03:29,065 --> 00:03:29,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure.

35
00:03:29,485 --> 00:03:42,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it's sometimes pleasant, sometimes elated, sometimes depressed, sometimes... All those are like phenomena in the same sense as you have things arising in front of your eyes, arising on their own, ceasing, persisting.

36
00:03:42,559 --> 00:03:48,165
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the internal phenomenon of your of your general mind, that ambiguous one, arises in the same manner.

37
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:51,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you haven't asked for... for elated mind.

38
00:03:51,291 --> 00:03:52,292
[SPEAKER_01]: It just arose like that.

39
00:03:52,712 --> 00:03:53,512
[SPEAKER_01]: And now it's present.

40
00:03:53,552 --> 00:03:54,153
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enduring.

41
00:03:54,193 --> 00:03:55,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It's persisting like that.

42
00:03:55,613 --> 00:04:00,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And while it's persisting in the state of elation, if you're mindful of it, you won't be taken by that.

43
00:04:01,717 --> 00:04:05,819
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't discern that phenomenon of that mind for what it is, you will be taken.

44
00:04:05,839 --> 00:04:06,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you'll be elated.

45
00:04:07,499 --> 00:04:12,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, inexplicably, inexplicably, but you'll be all over the place, because you're in a great mood.

46
00:04:12,622 --> 00:04:13,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is good.

47
00:04:13,663 --> 00:04:15,403
[SPEAKER_01]: All the nothing has changed, really.

48
00:04:15,423 --> 00:04:16,224
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just your mind.

49
00:04:17,204 --> 00:04:18,325
[SPEAKER_01]: But because you... you

50
00:04:18,832 --> 00:04:25,357
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no insight, or rather, you have no capacity, but to not take it as yours.

51
00:04:25,397 --> 00:04:27,579
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to take it as yours, because you don't know anything else.

52
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:28,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you are related.

53
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:32,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you're all affected, and everybody's in the same sense.

54
00:04:32,103 --> 00:04:45,914
[SPEAKER_01]: When that mind, that relation ceases, and then, another state, another mind arises, a mind of ill-will, or a mind of depression, or a mind of something, then, you're completely taken by that.

55
00:04:45,934 --> 00:04:47,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, you're completely

56
00:04:49,082 --> 00:04:51,843
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's elated, you know it is elated.

57
00:05:19,028 --> 00:05:20,629
[SPEAKER_01]: When he's depressed, you know he's depressed.

58
00:05:20,849 --> 00:05:23,031
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's actually enough to not be taken away.

59
00:05:23,531 --> 00:05:25,072
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the practice of satipaṭṭhāna.

60
00:05:25,532 --> 00:05:27,133
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows his mind is expanded.

61
00:05:27,173 --> 00:05:28,374
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows his mind is shrunk.

62
00:05:28,434 --> 00:05:29,635
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows his mind is elated.

63
00:05:29,655 --> 00:05:30,856
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows his mind is depressed.

64
00:05:31,176 --> 00:05:32,777
[SPEAKER_01]: If he's depressed, he knows he's depressed.

65
00:05:32,817 --> 00:05:34,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not I'm being depressed.

66
00:05:34,658 --> 00:05:36,940
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the presence of a depressed mind is there.

67
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:37,760
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

68
00:05:38,501 --> 00:05:41,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the presence of a different elated mind is there afterwards.

69
00:05:42,984 --> 00:05:46,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically... and it's not... and also, that's another assumption to assume that it's

70
00:05:48,472 --> 00:05:52,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all you're going to have is a state of your mind.

71
00:05:54,277 --> 00:05:57,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Your experience will always reveal your mind through a state.

72
00:05:58,581 --> 00:05:59,561
[SPEAKER_01]: And those states are different.

73
00:06:00,382 --> 00:06:03,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, those minds are minds.

74
00:06:04,325 --> 00:06:04,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Brutal.

75
00:06:05,726 --> 00:06:08,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at the same time, but basically,

76
00:06:10,018 --> 00:06:15,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of one mind, in that the utmost general level, means such and such mind is there.

77
00:06:16,102 --> 00:06:21,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, another presence of a different mind, opposite to that, means such and such mind is there.

78
00:06:21,025 --> 00:06:22,647
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all you can truly know.

79
00:06:23,127 --> 00:06:26,189
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume that it's the same mind that induces those states.

80
00:06:26,989 --> 00:06:36,356
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you see that there is no mind being discerned without those states given beforehand, you realize, oh, the mind is inseparable from those states.

81
00:06:36,456 --> 00:06:39,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the mind cannot be on its own, independent of them.

82
00:06:44,993 --> 00:06:46,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the same sense with the feelings.

83
00:06:46,414 --> 00:06:49,876
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha said, do you take feelings to be yours?

84
00:06:49,916 --> 00:06:50,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yes.

85
00:06:50,376 --> 00:06:52,377
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, well, which one do you take then to be yours?

86
00:06:52,997 --> 00:06:57,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is pleasant feeling, there is unpleasant feeling, there is neither pleasant nor pleasant.

87
00:06:57,339 --> 00:06:58,600
[SPEAKER_01]: They're completely different feelings.

88
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:00,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Separate, different feelings.

89
00:07:01,862 --> 00:07:08,545
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no one feeling that then gets, kind of, eh... turned into the colors of pleasure, then colors of displeasure.

90
00:07:08,705 --> 00:07:08,925
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

91
00:07:09,206 --> 00:07:10,286
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a different feeling.

92
00:07:10,426 --> 00:07:11,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Entirely different feeling.

93
00:07:12,921 --> 00:07:13,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And they don't mix.

94
00:07:13,802 --> 00:07:15,042
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't, like, connect.

95
00:07:15,082 --> 00:07:16,503
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't mold into each other.

96
00:07:17,084 --> 00:07:19,465
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have three different feelings, which one is yours, then?

97
00:07:20,165 --> 00:07:25,669
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have, like, millions of manifestations of your mind, which one is yours?

98
00:07:26,729 --> 00:07:26,969
[SPEAKER_01]: None.

99
00:07:27,270 --> 00:07:28,110
[SPEAKER_01]: None can be yours.

100
00:07:29,571 --> 00:07:34,594
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can see that, in general, the mind is, say, taken by a longing of yours.

101
00:07:34,614 --> 00:07:35,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

102
00:07:35,475 --> 00:07:37,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Genuinely, well, I should say,

103
00:07:38,683 --> 00:07:46,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Not taken by longing, because that implies the mind sits there, but you can see that the mind, which is a general, is the longing mind.

104
00:07:47,210 --> 00:07:48,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the depressed mind.

105
00:07:48,931 --> 00:07:49,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the happy mind.

106
00:07:50,352 --> 00:07:51,373
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the even mind.

107
00:07:51,433 --> 00:07:52,554
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the equanimous mind.

108
00:07:52,614 --> 00:07:53,175
[SPEAKER_01]: It's this mind.

109
00:07:53,195 --> 00:07:53,695
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that mind.

110
00:07:54,035 --> 00:07:54,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is there.

111
00:07:57,158 --> 00:08:06,125
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you see it as dependent, or rather as inseparable from the state it has arisen, well, you see that it's coming from over there, not from over here, i.e.

112
00:08:06,145 --> 00:08:06,345
[SPEAKER_01]: me.

113
00:08:07,742 --> 00:08:09,764
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are just sort of mindful of it.

114
00:08:09,804 --> 00:08:14,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It just arises, ceases and persists while it changes, as any other phenomenon does.

115
00:08:15,328 --> 00:08:19,732
[SPEAKER_01]: It shares exactly the same nature, but on that general level.

116
00:08:23,916 --> 00:08:25,397
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to abandon those things.

117
00:08:26,198 --> 00:08:34,645
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say abandon, if you see, if you know that this longing, you know, you have a longing mind, abandon longing.

118
00:08:34,665 --> 00:08:35,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, like,

119
00:08:37,088 --> 00:08:45,336
[SPEAKER_01]: You abandon longing, sure, but you don't abandon longing by interfering with the longing mind, because that wasn't yours to begin with.

120
00:08:46,597 --> 00:08:54,344
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can abandon you appropriating the longing, the mind that longs, the mind that's sad, the mind that's happy.

121
00:08:55,145 --> 00:08:56,426
[SPEAKER_01]: You abandon that.

122
00:08:57,207 --> 00:08:59,990
[SPEAKER_01]: You abandon the appropriation of that.

123
00:09:00,890 --> 00:09:02,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a very different thing.

124
00:09:05,721 --> 00:09:12,846
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say, oh, yes, you abandon the longing mind...

125
00:09:12,886 --> 00:09:17,849
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say you abandon the longing mind, that means, like, oh, you must remove the longing from your mind.

126
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:19,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

127
00:09:19,331 --> 00:09:20,051
[SPEAKER_01]: And that doesn't work.

128
00:09:20,331 --> 00:09:22,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, the mind is that longing.

129
00:09:24,154 --> 00:09:34,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So, removing longing from your mind means you still assume that the mind is separate from that longing, and then this longing just infected it, and then you clear it out, and then the pure mind stays.

130
00:09:35,002 --> 00:09:37,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the pure mind is the mind that's left alone.

131
00:09:40,044 --> 00:09:40,184
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

132
00:09:40,204 --> 00:09:48,326
[SPEAKER_00]: The pure mind is the mind which you don't get involved in.

133
00:09:50,227 --> 00:09:57,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you basically discern the impossibility of your involvement in the first place, which is how you cannot... which is how you stop being involved.

134
00:09:59,150 --> 00:10:01,971
[SPEAKER_00]: What about seeing the position in regard to the mind?

135
00:10:02,111 --> 00:10:02,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Your position.

136
00:10:04,492 --> 00:10:04,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning?

137
00:10:06,558 --> 00:10:13,401
[SPEAKER_00]: You see a mind as the background, and everything else is more particular.

138
00:10:13,421 --> 00:10:17,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see you're not the background.

139
00:10:18,704 --> 00:10:27,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that would allow a citizen stepping away from the background of the mind.

140
00:10:27,088 --> 00:10:29,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it could help.

141
00:10:29,949 --> 00:10:35,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Because usually people, by not seeing that, they're taken by that background,

142
00:10:35,651 --> 00:10:48,958
[SPEAKER_01]: kind of magnetic cloud, it sort of directs them towards ideal will, or sensuality, or whatever hindrance arose, or through, like, elated, super mood, happy, joyful, whatever.

143
00:10:49,178 --> 00:10:55,622
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's basically completely dependent on an afterly, randomly, circumstantially manifested mind.

144
00:11:01,149 --> 00:11:06,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you can basically be mindful throughout the day, regardless of where you are, what you do.

145
00:11:07,431 --> 00:11:08,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not completely what you do.

146
00:11:08,452 --> 00:11:10,012
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to practice restraint, obviously.

147
00:11:10,693 --> 00:11:16,115
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I mean, like, whatever you're doing, then it's not clearly unwholesome.

148
00:11:17,635 --> 00:11:28,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Just by keeping that peripheral eye on that general state of your mind, for what it is, you, you already, basically, you're already fortified against that, that, like,

149
00:11:31,065 --> 00:11:38,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure that that mind would exert on you to do particular things, so that the mind, like a general cloud of, like, sensual craving,

150
00:11:40,339 --> 00:11:52,143
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not, if you're not seeing it for what it is, as a phenomenon, it will eventually pressure you into particularizing that very craving through either, you know, engaging through any sensuality that you can get at the time, wherever you were.

151
00:11:52,703 --> 00:11:55,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Be it food, music, entertainment, whatever.

152
00:11:55,524 --> 00:11:56,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Things like that.

153
00:11:56,804 --> 00:12:03,146
[SPEAKER_01]: You end up going towards that because it pushed you there because you haven't been, you haven't been fortified in regard to it.

154
00:12:03,603 --> 00:12:06,505
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you remind, like, oh, it's still there.

155
00:12:06,925 --> 00:12:12,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, three hours later of you doing whatever, you look, oh, that central mind, it's still there.

156
00:12:12,968 --> 00:12:13,588
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enduring.

157
00:12:13,788 --> 00:12:14,389
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't matter.

158
00:12:14,429 --> 00:12:17,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it stays forever, it's still in common.

159
00:12:17,350 --> 00:12:18,911
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that it will have to cease.

160
00:12:20,372 --> 00:12:24,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it might, like, last forever, infinitely, so to speak.

161
00:12:24,094 --> 00:12:25,815
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no end in sight when you look at it.

162
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:30,541
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that it's arising, it manifests, means that's why it will cease.

163
00:12:30,841 --> 00:12:33,201
[SPEAKER_01]: So, infinite things are eternal.

164
00:12:33,841 --> 00:12:35,862
[SPEAKER_01]: They will come to an end, with their infinity.

165
00:12:36,562 --> 00:12:42,243
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the new thing arises, new mind arises, new infinity arises, and that infinity is also impermanent.

166
00:12:45,083 --> 00:12:47,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like two completely different domains.

167
00:12:48,484 --> 00:12:55,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, and I was thinking that everything has... well, these things that arise, always arise with a sense of...

168
00:12:56,338 --> 00:12:58,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

169
00:12:58,339 --> 00:12:59,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they arise with the infinity.

170
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,302
[SPEAKER_01]: They arise because there's no... there's no end in sight.

171
00:13:02,322 --> 00:13:04,823
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no end in terms of the possibilities.

172
00:13:04,843 --> 00:13:05,424
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no end.

173
00:13:05,744 --> 00:13:07,425
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what's mistaken as forever.

174
00:13:09,306 --> 00:13:12,148
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, you can have an infinite range of things.

175
00:13:13,409 --> 00:13:14,429
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that's taken away.

176
00:13:14,750 --> 00:13:17,191
[SPEAKER_01]: That you don't have an infinite range of things anymore.

177
00:13:17,211 --> 00:13:19,152
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how infinity is impermanent.

178
00:13:20,313 --> 00:13:22,234
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, I think I said that in one of my answers.

179
00:13:22,334 --> 00:13:24,716
[SPEAKER_01]: If, basically, if a person would be able to discern infinite,

180
00:13:25,920 --> 00:13:37,167
[SPEAKER_01]: From permanent... Or see... Well, discern the difference and then you will recognize, ah, actually, permanence comes from me, the assumption of permanence, the assumption of forever.

181
00:13:38,728 --> 00:13:45,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Infinite... it's kind of... everything is infinite in that sense, because everything arises with, like, infinite amount of possibilities.

182
00:13:48,434 --> 00:13:53,157
[SPEAKER_01]: When it arises, but when it ceases... it ceases with infinite amount of possibilities.

183
00:13:54,013 --> 00:13:57,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And a new thing is manifested, or rather, a different thing is manifested also.

184
00:13:58,095 --> 00:14:04,839
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything arises in that, like, infinite structure of possibilities, like infinite mind, infinite permutations.

185
00:14:04,899 --> 00:14:06,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there's no end to them.

186
00:14:06,640 --> 00:14:11,283
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be seeking out and trying to encompass them all, and you won't.

187
00:14:11,343 --> 00:14:15,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And you always will be more, and there'll always be more, to more, and more, to more, and more, to more, until it ceases.

188
00:14:18,353 --> 00:14:24,798
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole thing, the entire domain, the entire domain of infinity, from the past all the way into the future, that's impermanent.

189
00:14:25,138 --> 00:14:26,960
[SPEAKER_01]: That ceases.

190
00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:35,926
[SPEAKER_01]: On a particular level, yeah, things last forever, in that sense, in that infinite sense, until they cease, on the general level, which is where they cease.

191
00:14:37,868 --> 00:14:42,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's another mistake to look for impermanence in actual, like, in actual

192
00:14:43,666 --> 00:14:44,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of things.

193
00:14:44,727 --> 00:14:48,810
[SPEAKER_01]: When you look for them and you want to see their destruction and go, ah, that's impermanent.

194
00:14:48,890 --> 00:14:49,070
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

195
00:14:49,190 --> 00:14:52,533
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just another arisen thing with infinite possibility.

196
00:14:53,053 --> 00:14:57,416
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just witnessing a phenomenon, an arisen phenomenon of destruction.

197
00:14:58,737 --> 00:14:59,558
[SPEAKER_01]: And that has arisen.

198
00:15:00,919 --> 00:15:08,344
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I mean, when I say before, or I say often, things are not impermanent because you see them cease.

199
00:15:09,545 --> 00:15:13,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are impermanent because they have arisen in the first place.

200
00:15:15,422 --> 00:15:19,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So, their manifestation means that's why they're not in your control.

201
00:15:20,483 --> 00:15:21,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are manifesting.

202
00:15:22,864 --> 00:15:25,905
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive you creating anything.

203
00:15:26,565 --> 00:15:27,945
[SPEAKER_01]: You manifesting anything.

204
00:15:29,025 --> 00:15:29,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

205
00:15:32,726 --> 00:15:41,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And even when you think of you creating something, if you look closely, whatever you are kind of working with in your mind, in terms of you, that was given beforehand.

206
00:15:43,229 --> 00:15:44,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you always come in second.

207
00:16:15,523 --> 00:16:16,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it's so.

208
00:16:17,578 --> 00:16:29,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning defilements of mind, whichever defilement, you abandon it by not taking it as yours anymore.

209
00:16:31,083 --> 00:16:46,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, just before you go further, defilements will basically refer then to the whole range of that generally affected peripheral cloud, the background of your mind, that's affected by your sensuality, all the way up into

210
00:16:49,061 --> 00:17:01,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Particular push, that's pushing in a particular direction, or, you know, those... finding something like a... like a... like a particular manifestation of that general pressure, like vague ambiguous central craving that you find.

211
00:17:01,172 --> 00:17:01,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

212
00:17:02,233 --> 00:17:05,856
[SPEAKER_01]: You sort of centralize in this particular thing, or that particular thing.

213
00:17:06,116 --> 00:17:07,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that whole range will be defined.

214
00:17:08,819 --> 00:17:11,501
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can start saying no to these particular things.

215
00:17:11,801 --> 00:17:12,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

216
00:17:12,262 --> 00:17:13,223
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the first step.

217
00:17:13,941 --> 00:17:22,087
[SPEAKER_01]: But you should do that only so that you can then start discerning the phenomenon of your mind, a reason on its own, being affected by such and such unwholesome state.

218
00:17:23,568 --> 00:17:29,112
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just go around denying the things in front of you, fine, but you're carrying the very source of the problem with you.

219
00:17:29,132 --> 00:17:30,053
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not addressing it.

220
00:17:32,173 --> 00:17:34,994
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't get involved with that...

221
00:17:35,434 --> 00:17:45,458
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, seeing it for what it is, seeing, grasping that sign of that phenomenon, of that mind that has arisen in such-and-such way, means you're not getting involved, because you get involved by not seeing it.

222
00:17:47,819 --> 00:17:56,803
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the first... well, not the first, but one of the requisites, pre-requisite for Sotāpārthi, as Janabiru mentioned, was grasping the sign of your mind.

223
00:17:58,009 --> 00:18:04,294
[SPEAKER_01]: cittanimitta, like grasping that phenomenon of that ambiguous, vague cloud of the mind for what it is.

224
00:18:04,714 --> 00:18:05,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Not over-defining it.

225
00:18:05,995 --> 00:18:06,175
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

226
00:18:06,315 --> 00:18:07,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, this is what my mind is.

227
00:18:07,956 --> 00:18:08,157
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

228
00:18:08,177 --> 00:18:10,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Just seeing it for what it is, the way it has arisen.

229
00:18:10,939 --> 00:18:27,411
[SPEAKER_01]: When you start seeing it for, like, the way it has arisen on its own, and not, not adding more to it, not taking away from it, then, that phenomenon, because it's seen for what it is, can then be understood as, as, as not mind.

230
00:18:28,404 --> 00:18:31,425
[SPEAKER_01]: But whenever there is a form of involvement, then that means appropriation.

231
00:18:31,465 --> 00:18:39,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you add something to it, if you take away from it, means you still assume that you can add something to it, that you can take away something.

232
00:18:39,207 --> 00:18:39,427
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

233
00:18:39,567 --> 00:18:40,227
[SPEAKER_01]: You are involved.

234
00:18:40,927 --> 00:18:44,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you're not seeing the clear sign of your mind.

235
00:18:44,388 --> 00:18:45,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you have to make it clearer.

236
00:18:45,968 --> 00:18:47,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you see it for what it is?

237
00:18:47,569 --> 00:18:50,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Arising, ceasing, persisting or changing.

238
00:18:51,230 --> 00:18:52,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, persisting or changing.

239
00:18:52,790 --> 00:18:55,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Because arising is persisting or changing.

240
00:18:55,351 --> 00:18:57,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Ceasing away is persisting or changing.

241
00:18:58,202 --> 00:19:01,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, just basically seeing the given state of mind as not yours.

242
00:19:01,943 --> 00:19:10,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and you have to see that repetitively, like repetitively, on and on, because your first day, you get a clearer picture, you might see it first time, but then you're like, oh, I saw it once and that was it.

243
00:19:10,625 --> 00:19:14,245
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you have to keep seeing it, and seeing it, and seeing it, for how long?

244
00:19:14,265 --> 00:19:19,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it depends how well established the assumption of mind was, in regard to that mind.

245
00:19:20,046 --> 00:19:21,807
[SPEAKER_01]: For some people it's more, for some people it's less.

246
00:19:25,027 --> 00:19:27,708
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think, given that it's two and a half millennia since

247
00:19:28,628 --> 00:19:33,409
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha died and the kind of pure dhamma disappeared.

248
00:19:33,529 --> 00:19:35,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It's probably more for all the people.

249
00:19:36,230 --> 00:19:40,831
[SPEAKER_01]: They need to do lots of undoing for those assumptions to disappear.

250
00:19:40,871 --> 00:19:48,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, the universal principles of dhamma, the path that the Buddha himself only discovered, did not create, is always there.

251
00:19:49,593 --> 00:19:58,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if there's no Buddha around, no dhamma around, if somebody's repeating these things correctly, they would reach the same goal.

252
00:19:58,668 --> 00:20:01,090
[SPEAKER_01]: They would, apparently, procreation.

253
00:20:01,110 --> 00:20:02,350
[SPEAKER_01]: They would see mind for what it is.

254
00:20:03,431 --> 00:20:19,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, and then, they would say that, and say, like, um, like, even purtutian view, people who don't have the right view, who never heard, like, who are not established in the right discipline, right disposition, and so on, uh, even they could actually experience this passion, this enchantment towards this body.

255
00:20:19,701 --> 00:20:19,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

256
00:20:19,962 --> 00:20:28,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, I mean, we see bodies disgusting, oozing, uh, getting sick, liable to misfortune, just anything, and then see other people dying, and so on.

257
00:20:28,807 --> 00:20:35,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you dwell on it long enough, you will experience revulsion and disenchantment and disappearance of passion in regard to your body.

258
00:20:36,153 --> 00:20:44,119
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's hard, and it's almost impossible, without the right view and the right instruction, to experience the same disenchantment towards your own mind.

259
00:20:44,840 --> 00:20:48,583
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in the same sense, basically, you can see that you can.

260
00:20:48,863 --> 00:20:52,726
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you can experience dispassion towards your mind is also a phenomenon.

261
00:20:53,287 --> 00:20:57,130
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, your body is a phenomenon towards which you can experience that dispassion.

262
00:20:58,338 --> 00:21:06,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he said, it's hard to experience this special one, because the mind is much quicker, much more ambiguous, much more vague, much more fleeting, changes quickly, moves around, so you don't see it.

263
00:21:07,803 --> 00:21:16,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And by not seeing it, you're appropriating it, and you have been appropriating it for such a long time, that it's almost, like, well, it feels almost as if impenetrable.

264
00:21:18,869 --> 00:21:20,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there is no more to it.

265
00:21:20,209 --> 00:21:22,470
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just taken for granted that the mind is mine.

266
00:21:23,431 --> 00:21:24,291
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is mine.

267
00:21:24,311 --> 00:21:25,252
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again,

268
00:21:28,672 --> 00:21:35,175
[SPEAKER_01]: With the proper instruction, in terms of the dharmic practice, then you could start discerning even that mind as a phenomenon, no matter how quick it is.

269
00:21:36,095 --> 00:21:45,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Generally, it stays the phenomenon of the mind, so you don't... you're not swayed by the particular movements of the general mind, general moods, because they will always remain general.

270
00:21:48,381 --> 00:21:51,443
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eventually, you can abandon even passion,

271
00:21:53,225 --> 00:21:55,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Enchantment or whatever else with that as well.

272
00:21:55,586 --> 00:22:00,248
[SPEAKER_01]: It just becomes another thing that's arising, ceasing, existing, world-changing.

273
00:22:06,031 --> 00:22:06,871
[SPEAKER_01]: I never meant for this.

274
00:22:08,372 --> 00:22:09,032
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it, boy?

275
00:22:09,632 --> 00:22:09,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Huh?

276
00:22:19,096 --> 00:22:19,437
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

277
00:22:20,137 --> 00:22:20,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Six.

278
00:22:33,813 --> 00:22:47,284
[SPEAKER_00]: It always follows us, okay, to see if your mind has... what kind of state your mind is in.

279
00:22:47,745 --> 00:22:48,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it defiled or not?

280
00:22:49,746 --> 00:22:53,750
[SPEAKER_00]: If it is, abandon that defiled mind.

281
00:22:54,270 --> 00:22:56,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, even if it isn't abandoned,

282
00:22:57,516 --> 00:23:01,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's not a defilement, even if it's happiness and good mood, don't take it to be yours.

283
00:23:03,659 --> 00:23:12,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, seeing the mind regardless of what that mind is, whether it's defiled or not defiled.

284
00:23:13,102 --> 00:23:18,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, don't entertain the defilements further, when they are present in the general mind.

285
00:23:18,645 --> 00:23:20,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, by all means.

286
00:23:21,666 --> 00:23:23,907
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's happiness, well, again, be wary of that.

287
00:23:23,927 --> 00:23:26,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, there's that sutta, I think,

288
00:23:27,868 --> 00:23:42,694
[SPEAKER_01]: I think Sariputra, I don't know if Sariputra said, one should regard pleasure as like a painful arrow, dangerous arrow, and then pain as like a deadly.

289
00:23:43,815 --> 00:23:47,096
[SPEAKER_01]: If pain is a dot, pleasure is a dot.

290
00:23:47,176 --> 00:23:49,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure is a dot, pain is a deadly arrow, or something like that.

291
00:23:50,637 --> 00:23:57,060
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sure, pleasure is not harmful, not unwholesome, but it's dangerous if you don't see your mind in regard to it.

292
00:23:57,855 --> 00:24:02,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, then, when another mind comes, you won't see that either.

293
00:24:06,219 --> 00:24:12,142
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't, like, oh, well, you know, I look at stuff that bothers me, but I'm not going to look at stuff because it shares the same nature.

294
00:24:12,762 --> 00:24:16,064
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can abandon what bothers you only by abandoning the nature.

295
00:24:16,534 --> 00:24:17,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Of the entire thing.

296
00:24:17,555 --> 00:24:21,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And that thing also includes the nature of happy mind, or elated mind, or good mind.

297
00:24:22,257 --> 00:24:24,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't get taken... you don't get elated by it.

298
00:24:25,079 --> 00:24:25,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

299
00:24:25,599 --> 00:24:26,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, elation is there.

300
00:24:26,540 --> 00:24:26,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

301
00:24:26,900 --> 00:24:33,704
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have to do anything about it, but I certainly can also not get involved with it, not get taken by it.

302
00:24:34,084 --> 00:24:44,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say, like, to... when he knows his mind is unstained or undefiled, then joy arises.

303
00:24:45,996 --> 00:24:46,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's different.

304
00:24:46,976 --> 00:25:02,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's like a type of joy that arises when... not like the joy that comes as a kind of cloud of your mind, not as a state of your mind, but a joy that arises in regard to knowing your mind.

305
00:25:03,500 --> 00:25:14,502
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, again, as Sariputta said, there is a pleasure arising in regard to not feeling any pleasure nor pain for an arahant, and that's very pleasant.

306
00:25:15,342 --> 00:25:19,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I think that the joy of the mind is undefiled.

307
00:25:19,623 --> 00:25:20,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

308
00:25:20,203 --> 00:25:27,744
[SPEAKER_01]: You should cultivate joy of knowing your mind, which is a joy that doesn't depend on the state of your mind, doesn't depend on your mind full stop.

309
00:25:27,924 --> 00:25:31,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what it's... that's what I really... that's what I'm saying.

310
00:25:31,845 --> 00:25:32,545
[SPEAKER_00]: I want the joy...

311
00:25:32,565 --> 00:25:33,665
[SPEAKER_00]: I want the joy part.

312
00:25:33,805 --> 00:25:34,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

313
00:25:34,265 --> 00:25:34,586
[SPEAKER_00]: I want...

314
00:25:34,766 --> 00:25:38,066
[SPEAKER_00]: I want the defilement to be replaced.

315
00:25:38,506 --> 00:25:39,386
[SPEAKER_00]: By the joy?

316
00:25:39,426 --> 00:25:40,487
[SPEAKER_00]: By the joy of mind.

317
00:25:40,867 --> 00:25:42,567
[SPEAKER_01]: You can replace it with joy,

318
00:25:43,847 --> 00:25:47,310
[SPEAKER_01]: You can replace both defilements and joy, joyful mind.

319
00:25:48,651 --> 00:25:49,412
[SPEAKER_01]: You replace that.

320
00:25:50,092 --> 00:25:58,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, by abandoning that, you experience joy that doesn't depend neither on defilements, nor on your mind, nor on anything, basically, that's impermanent.

321
00:25:59,440 --> 00:26:02,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why that pleasure is not unwholesome.

322
00:26:03,363 --> 00:26:05,324
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like the pleasure of the jhānas, similar.

323
00:26:06,245 --> 00:26:10,429
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because it's not unwholesome, fine, you don't have to worry about it, you can even cultivate it.

324
00:26:11,241 --> 00:26:18,367
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not unwholesome, because it's not underlined by by the, like, latent tendencies towards craving, towards this.

325
00:26:21,610 --> 00:26:22,511
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's different.

326
00:26:22,531 --> 00:26:23,211
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a joy.

327
00:26:23,872 --> 00:26:30,257
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not happiness that has arisen on account of a happy mind, because the mind is erected.

328
00:26:30,698 --> 00:26:31,759
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a happiness that has arisen.

329
00:26:31,779 --> 00:26:31,879
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

330
00:26:32,379 --> 00:26:36,863
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a happiness that has arisen in regard to knowing what mind

331
00:26:37,296 --> 00:26:43,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, to recognizing the mind, to seeing the phenomenon of that mind, to not being moved by it, to being stronger than that mind.

332
00:26:44,025 --> 00:26:51,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the big heavy cloud of that mind, be it good or bad, wants to take you, wants to pressure you, wants to make you, you know, go a certain way.

333
00:26:52,216 --> 00:26:54,399
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will, for as long as that cloud is stronger than you.

334
00:26:55,937 --> 00:27:06,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you become stronger, you... and then it's like the simile in the Terra Terrigata, you know, it means like a mountain, and no matter what clouds and storms come, it just does not move.

335
00:27:06,785 --> 00:27:07,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

336
00:27:07,425 --> 00:27:09,807
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean there are no storms and clouds coming.

337
00:27:10,367 --> 00:27:12,088
[SPEAKER_01]: On the contrary, it means it just doesn't move.

338
00:27:12,529 --> 00:27:18,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's another sutra which would have said, eh... arahant... for... arahant is not overwhelmed by his mind.

339
00:27:19,133 --> 00:27:21,555
[SPEAKER_01]: He is the one who overwhelms his mind.

340
00:27:23,756 --> 00:27:24,017
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

341
00:27:25,550 --> 00:27:32,355
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop in regard to... and you can only start developing in regard to your mind when you grasp the sign of that mind.

342
00:27:32,795 --> 00:27:34,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is what the mind is.

343
00:27:35,757 --> 00:27:37,058
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nimitta of the mind.

344
00:27:37,178 --> 00:27:39,039
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you start developing in regard to that.

345
00:27:39,079 --> 00:27:48,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about the defilements never arising again, but it's the assumption that the defilements of mind or the mind is the mind.

346
00:27:48,565 --> 00:27:49,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

347
00:27:49,286 --> 00:27:52,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Defilements are defilements only when they're affecting you.

348
00:27:53,008 --> 00:27:55,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Only when you're taken by them, when you took them...

349
00:27:55,271 --> 00:27:57,033
[SPEAKER_01]: But there will still be defilements.

350
00:27:57,053 --> 00:28:01,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that defile will still arise, but they will not defile you.

351
00:28:01,659 --> 00:28:02,480
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a different thing.

352
00:28:03,081 --> 00:28:06,725
[SPEAKER_01]: You still have mud and dirt, but now you're not getting dirty by it.

353
00:28:07,386 --> 00:28:08,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Before, you would.

354
00:28:08,327 --> 00:28:09,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever there was mud, you were muddy.

355
00:28:10,130 --> 00:28:11,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever there was dirt, you were dirty.

356
00:28:12,141 --> 00:28:15,022
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you've cleaned your mind and it can't get dirty.

357
00:28:15,363 --> 00:28:21,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, the simile, like the lotus on top of a horrible swamp, filthy pond, not touched by it.

358
00:28:21,626 --> 00:28:26,068
[SPEAKER_00]: But also the... when the Buddha meets Mara, Mara always comes around.

359
00:28:26,148 --> 00:28:27,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the Mara still kept coming.

360
00:28:28,509 --> 00:28:31,051
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha just, like, always see him.

361
00:28:31,491 --> 00:28:31,691
[SPEAKER_01]: i.e.

362
00:28:31,711 --> 00:28:32,872
[SPEAKER_01]: he would always discern.

363
00:28:33,992 --> 00:28:37,634
[SPEAKER_01]: There wouldn't be even a remote possibility that he might not be able to see him.

364
00:28:37,674 --> 00:28:37,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

365
00:28:37,854 --> 00:28:39,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he penetrated the whole nature.

366
00:28:39,615 --> 00:28:40,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And this, for the Narada, is the same.

367
00:28:41,810 --> 00:28:43,391
[SPEAKER_01]: He understood the full meaning of abandonment.

368
00:28:43,451 --> 00:28:46,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter what arises, it can never defile him again.

369
00:28:47,933 --> 00:28:49,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there are no defilements.

370
00:28:50,014 --> 00:28:55,817
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yes, the storm clouds and stuff will keep arising, because it was arising with something to begin with.

371
00:28:55,937 --> 00:28:57,498
[SPEAKER_01]: It has nothing to do with you from the start.

372
00:28:57,998 --> 00:29:00,019
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said that, actually, in regard to Mara.

373
00:29:00,059 --> 00:29:04,802
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, the reason why Mara can do with people what he wants, i.e.

374
00:29:04,922 --> 00:29:08,324
[SPEAKER_01]: push them in the directions and just make them engage in stuff,

375
00:29:09,036 --> 00:29:12,798
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because they take that which is his.

376
00:29:12,938 --> 00:29:14,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you go there in his domain.

377
00:29:14,859 --> 00:29:16,540
[SPEAKER_01]: You yourself go in his domain.

378
00:29:16,980 --> 00:29:20,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can't complain for him doing what he wants with you.

379
00:29:20,782 --> 00:29:26,384
[SPEAKER_01]: You put on the suit and I think you... Well, he said... And what is it that belongs to Māra that people use?

380
00:29:26,484 --> 00:29:30,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, eye and sights, ear and sounds.

381
00:29:30,706 --> 00:29:32,587
[SPEAKER_01]: No, basically everything.

382
00:29:33,247 --> 00:29:34,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Your whole sense is everything.

383
00:29:34,548 --> 00:29:35,028
[SPEAKER_01]: In and out.

384
00:29:35,889 --> 00:29:36,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Belongs to Māra.

385
00:29:40,380 --> 00:29:42,441
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a bit of tension, isn't it?

386
00:29:42,521 --> 00:29:42,701
Eh?

387
00:29:49,225 --> 00:29:49,785
[SPEAKER_01]: In the business.

388
00:29:52,166 --> 00:29:53,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because there is this...

389
00:29:54,888 --> 00:29:56,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Does anger still exist in Buddha?

390
00:29:57,689 --> 00:29:58,289
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

391
00:29:58,629 --> 00:30:00,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's not his, but it's anger.

392
00:30:01,451 --> 00:30:06,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it wouldn't... like, you can't... you can't use the same designation or the same term anger.

393
00:30:06,794 --> 00:30:08,975
[SPEAKER_01]: If you use it as anger for Putujana, i.e.

394
00:30:10,545 --> 00:30:16,688
[SPEAKER_01]: A state that he's been defiled by, and it's making... and it's resulting in anger, you can't use the same for the Buddha.

395
00:30:17,848 --> 00:30:24,552
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say that things that would give rise to anger, still arise for the Buddha, but he's not angry.

396
00:30:25,072 --> 00:30:26,933
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't give rise to actual anger.

397
00:30:28,454 --> 00:30:37,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when they're out there trying to kill him and stuff, everybody would... anybody would be angry, or not a Buddha at all, or be upset, or feel ill will, but he didn't.

398
00:30:39,044 --> 00:30:39,264
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

399
00:30:39,404 --> 00:30:42,046
[SPEAKER_01]: And that doesn't mean that he was oblivious to what Devadatta was doing.

400
00:30:42,066 --> 00:30:43,347
[SPEAKER_01]: He was saying, he's a foolish man.

401
00:30:43,827 --> 00:30:44,827
[SPEAKER_01]: He's an idiot, basically.

402
00:30:45,588 --> 00:30:49,730
[SPEAKER_01]: His behavior is going to lead to, like, a long, long lasting harm for him, and so on.

403
00:30:50,491 --> 00:30:51,932
[SPEAKER_01]: But he wasn't angry on account of it.

404
00:30:51,992 --> 00:30:52,772
[SPEAKER_01]: He couldn't have been.

405
00:30:54,453 --> 00:30:57,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those defile... those things that defile are still there.

406
00:30:58,595 --> 00:30:59,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's Mahārāja's domain.

407
00:31:00,196 --> 00:31:03,939
[SPEAKER_01]: And they will remain there for as long as these senses are in his body.

408
00:31:04,319 --> 00:31:05,760
[SPEAKER_01]: These five aggregates don't break apart.

409
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,121
[SPEAKER_01]: When he reaches final nibbāna, then it's done.

410
00:31:11,168 --> 00:31:14,030
[SPEAKER_00]: What's it, Rebecca?

411
00:31:14,310 --> 00:31:15,951
[SPEAKER_00]: The joy.

412
00:31:15,971 --> 00:31:23,456
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows that he has abandoned the state of longing and joy.

413
00:31:24,196 --> 00:31:24,657
[SPEAKER_00]: He's there.

414
00:31:25,097 --> 00:31:26,498
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, rapture.

415
00:31:26,518 --> 00:31:32,082
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, his body is calm and his mind is concentrated.

416
00:31:33,683 --> 00:31:34,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

417
00:31:35,464 --> 00:31:37,245
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't say he was...

418
00:31:37,265 --> 00:31:38,226
[SPEAKER_00]: It says he's rapturous.

419
00:31:39,194 --> 00:31:42,237
[SPEAKER_00]: You always get the sense of, like, delighted, you're getting all this excitement.

420
00:31:42,317 --> 00:31:43,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not, yeah.

421
00:31:43,718 --> 00:31:45,400
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's a translation.

422
00:31:45,440 --> 00:31:47,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

423
00:31:47,142 --> 00:31:49,524
[SPEAKER_01]: It's sukha, or pity.

424
00:31:50,305 --> 00:31:56,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, which was translated with rapture, but I think rapture comes from a Christian, like, concept, rapture.

425
00:31:57,442 --> 00:32:02,545
[SPEAKER_01]: When the divine, you know, touches you, whatever, then you have that bodily elation of like, oh, rapture.

426
00:32:02,785 --> 00:32:03,645
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not.

427
00:32:03,886 --> 00:32:10,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can see through, like, well, you can get a glimpse of it through discerning the sign of your mind.

428
00:32:10,949 --> 00:32:14,972
[SPEAKER_01]: You can see that it leads to that kind of immovability, equanimity.

429
00:32:15,152 --> 00:32:15,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

430
00:32:15,632 --> 00:32:17,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like indifference.

431
00:32:17,253 --> 00:32:20,655
[SPEAKER_01]: No, indifference tells you you're cruel or oblivious or blind.

432
00:32:20,675 --> 00:32:22,796
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means nothing can move you.

433
00:32:24,884 --> 00:32:25,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And then that...

434
00:32:25,584 --> 00:32:38,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, you can also see through there, that through discernment of the phenomenon of your mind, then you start feeling content and joy that you are the one overwhelming your mind, not your mind overwhelming.

435
00:32:38,491 --> 00:32:44,754
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what arises, no matter how high you are, it's always chopping up your limbs and brutalizing you in every possible way.

436
00:32:45,707 --> 00:32:47,708
[SPEAKER_01]: You still overwhelm that mind.

437
00:32:48,289 --> 00:32:49,669
[SPEAKER_01]: That mind doesn't overwhelm you.

438
00:32:50,550 --> 00:32:54,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's some like extreme development, strength and joy.

439
00:32:54,873 --> 00:33:00,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And above all, you would, you would, like, well, you would know that.

440
00:33:01,296 --> 00:33:10,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see that in that sutta when it comes to, like, through knowing your mind, you experience that joy and then that results automatically in a concentration.

441
00:33:11,763 --> 00:33:16,090
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't get concentration through watching your nostrils or something.

442
00:33:16,130 --> 00:33:22,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you kind of develop this concentration and kind of all... No, you get concentration through discernment of right things.

443
00:33:24,343 --> 00:33:25,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by all means, yeah.

444
00:33:26,180 --> 00:33:35,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Meditate and sit cross-legged, or whatever, to discern what your mind... the sign of your mind, the clouds of your mind, the movement, or whatever.

445
00:33:35,410 --> 00:33:40,456
[SPEAKER_01]: See this phenomenon and fortify yourself in regard to it, and you'll be concentrated automatically.

446
00:33:40,836 --> 00:33:45,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you go to a village, whether you go to town, whether you sit alone, whether you live on top of a hill, your mind is established.

447
00:33:47,044 --> 00:33:47,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Step one.

448
00:33:47,985 --> 00:33:49,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandon the formless thing.

449
00:33:49,606 --> 00:33:49,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

450
00:33:49,946 --> 00:33:50,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I've done that.

451
00:33:50,927 --> 00:33:51,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

452
00:33:51,708 --> 00:33:53,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I'll come for the next session at 2.30.

453
00:33:53,269 --> 00:33:54,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I'll develop joy.

454
00:33:54,550 --> 00:33:54,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

455
00:33:54,971 --> 00:33:55,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

456
00:33:55,291 --> 00:33:56,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, hold on to that joy.

457
00:33:56,292 --> 00:33:57,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's have a tea break.

458
00:33:57,272 --> 00:33:57,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

459
00:33:57,653 --> 00:33:58,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, come back at 2.30.

460
00:33:58,954 --> 00:34:00,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, we can continue from that joy.

461
00:34:00,435 --> 00:34:01,516
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll lead you to another step.

462
00:34:02,837 --> 00:34:04,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's wishful thinking.

463
00:34:04,598 --> 00:34:06,280
[SPEAKER_01]: If it would only be like that.

464
00:34:06,460 --> 00:34:12,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Just here is a set... prescribed set of motions and little methods you do and you get there.

465
00:34:13,770 --> 00:34:18,393
[SPEAKER_01]: The only set, the only the only method is develop understanding of the wrong things.

466
00:34:19,834 --> 00:34:21,415
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you do, it will result in that.

467
00:34:21,995 --> 00:34:38,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Just think, because abandoning the mind, state of mind, I can say I want to abandon the defilements, but then I want I want to hold on to the to the joy, to the happiness.

468
00:34:38,645 --> 00:34:39,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

469
00:34:39,725 --> 00:34:42,287
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's often why, yeah, that's often why people

470
00:34:45,056 --> 00:34:45,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience fear.

471
00:34:45,617 --> 00:34:53,511
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, because you think, oh, well, if I... like, sure, I want to go in pain, but that means I have to give up all these joys and pleasures of the life.

472
00:34:54,693 --> 00:34:55,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Forever.

473
00:34:55,314 --> 00:34:56,056
[SPEAKER_01]: And it threatens them.

474
00:34:57,060 --> 00:34:57,600
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's true.

475
00:34:57,640 --> 00:34:58,180
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you...

476
00:34:58,400 --> 00:34:58,840
[SPEAKER_00]: It's true.

477
00:34:59,040 --> 00:35:00,921
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to abandon it as your... Yeah.

478
00:35:01,261 --> 00:35:02,601
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to abandon the whole thing.

479
00:35:02,961 --> 00:35:08,962
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean that you become joyless or... That means you discover a different type of happiness.

480
00:35:09,063 --> 00:35:10,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's again what the Buddha said.

481
00:35:10,823 --> 00:35:14,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, replace the household happiness, i.e.

482
00:35:14,384 --> 00:35:20,605
[SPEAKER_01]: the happiness of sensuality, the happiness of a lay life, replace that with happiness of renunciation.

483
00:35:21,636 --> 00:35:22,596
[SPEAKER_01]: And same with the pain.

484
00:35:23,097 --> 00:35:31,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Replace the pains, the bothers, the troubles of a lay life, with pains of renunciation, of an ascetic life.

485
00:35:32,621 --> 00:35:34,721
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more wholesome, it's much more pleasant.

486
00:35:36,482 --> 00:35:38,363
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, you can stop and think about it.

487
00:35:38,463 --> 00:35:50,448
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, after being a monk for a number of years, living relatively securely and so on, maybe we can... Yeah, you know, there are obviously hardships of physical kind and all sorts of things, dangers, whatever.

488
00:35:52,123 --> 00:36:03,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing is actually comparable to stuff you had to deal with as a layperson, when you were still in the world, with the job, and in stress, and just in the swamp.

489
00:36:04,616 --> 00:36:10,822
[SPEAKER_01]: The swamp of emotional issues, and duties, and burdens.

490
00:36:12,203 --> 00:36:13,645
[SPEAKER_01]: And that just accumulates.

491
00:36:16,665 --> 00:36:20,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you're 15, 20, 30 years old, then you just keep growing.

492
00:36:21,027 --> 00:36:22,207
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's there.

493
00:36:22,347 --> 00:36:24,328
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you have the burden.

494
00:36:24,348 --> 00:36:25,789
[SPEAKER_01]: You lay down the burden.

495
00:36:25,849 --> 00:36:33,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, that burden extends to all the previous lives and all the ones that are in store for you if you don't get it out.

496
00:36:33,612 --> 00:36:34,052
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is.

497
00:36:34,152 --> 00:36:36,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, oh, it's actually... yeah.

498
00:36:37,112 --> 00:36:38,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, again, teneteri gātau.

499
00:36:38,653 --> 00:36:42,195
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more pleasant here, living with these wild tigers and stuff.

500
00:36:42,215 --> 00:36:43,596
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't mind dying next moment.

501
00:36:44,217 --> 00:36:45,237
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's just clean.

502
00:36:46,158 --> 00:36:47,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotionally clean.

503
00:36:48,199 --> 00:36:49,380
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not burdening you.

504
00:36:49,420 --> 00:36:49,900
[SPEAKER_01]: It's free.

505
00:36:50,581 --> 00:36:51,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's freedom, in that sense.

506
00:36:52,742 --> 00:36:54,043
[SPEAKER_01]: But that takes time to develop.

507
00:36:55,044 --> 00:37:02,268
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind is used to the swamp of sensuality, distractions and so on, this seems like death.

508
00:37:02,708 --> 00:37:04,850
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why people freak out when they hear the Buddha.

509
00:37:06,052 --> 00:37:13,617
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to develop and fortify your mind, and become accustomed to it, then you start seeing benefits, and it's like, oh, no, this is the freedom.

510
00:37:14,697 --> 00:37:18,340
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, freedom, for those who are unaware, it's basically death.

511
00:37:20,101 --> 00:37:21,182
[SPEAKER_01]: They wouldn't be able to hold it.

512
00:37:21,202 --> 00:37:29,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because there's nothing else to be... you only used to be involved with things.

513
00:37:29,467 --> 00:37:29,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

514
00:37:29,907 --> 00:37:33,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, you become intentionally dependent upon involvement.

515
00:37:34,350 --> 00:37:34,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

516
00:37:35,286 --> 00:37:42,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, when there is nothing, it's just like boredom sets in, and then boredom gets replaced by a deep, deep anxiety and dread.

517
00:37:42,449 --> 00:37:50,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, quickly, find me something that will keep my mind away from me, from my own state, because I'll sink in it.

518
00:37:50,032 --> 00:37:54,954
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know the correct path, then even then boredom... Well, there is no boredom.

519
00:37:54,974 --> 00:38:04,558
[SPEAKER_01]: You go through it and it doesn't arise in me, because boredom only arises in regard to, like, accustomed levels of distraction.

520
00:38:05,790 --> 00:38:07,971
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when they are absent, then you feel bored.

521
00:38:08,751 --> 00:38:13,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, yes, a kind of like a gentle, unpleasant sort of push towards going back there.

522
00:38:13,713 --> 00:38:19,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And boredom is that neutral feeling which is, when unknown, it's unpleasant.

523
00:38:21,096 --> 00:38:22,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, now, there's nothing.

524
00:38:22,396 --> 00:38:24,577
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing exciting nor not exciting.

525
00:38:24,677 --> 00:38:25,177
[SPEAKER_01]: It's even.

526
00:38:25,877 --> 00:38:33,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But because you got used to only the excitement, that evenness is unpleasant now.

527
00:38:34,025 --> 00:38:35,246
[SPEAKER_01]: And you go back to the excitement.

528
00:38:35,686 --> 00:38:36,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the depression.

529
00:38:37,067 --> 00:38:38,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the depression, for that matter.

530
00:38:38,589 --> 00:38:41,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Depression is only depression in regard to things that you are depressed.

531
00:38:43,492 --> 00:38:52,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's again, like, when you do know that unpleasant... that neutral feeling, boredom or depression, when you know it, it's not unpleasant anymore.

532
00:38:53,440 --> 00:38:56,743
[SPEAKER_01]: When you discern it, it's quite peaceful.

533
00:39:00,106 --> 00:39:01,687
[SPEAKER_01]: But it takes time to develop.

534
00:39:02,166 --> 00:39:04,167
[SPEAKER_01]: To discern that criteria of what peace is.

535
00:39:07,729 --> 00:39:10,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, not get scared of it and think it's... Yeah.

536
00:39:10,390 --> 00:39:11,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

537
00:39:11,131 --> 00:39:20,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Get scared of it, because you... you're so... you became so dependent on that, like, constant movement, constant action, constant something.

538
00:39:21,076 --> 00:39:21,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Good or bad.

539
00:39:21,736 --> 00:39:23,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as long as something is happening.

540
00:39:23,837 --> 00:39:25,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, I'll just drown.

541
00:39:25,198 --> 00:39:31,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pretty much everyone's life out there.

542
00:39:34,951 --> 00:39:40,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you... if you think you're in control, well, okay, just... just... just decide to stop.

543
00:39:41,376 --> 00:39:50,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Just decide to stop engaging, stop giving in to distraction, and then you'll see what's going to arise, and for how long you'll be able to... to hold it, to hold your determination.

544
00:39:52,645 --> 00:39:55,387
[SPEAKER_01]: And the thing is, it's not really optional to do this.

545
00:39:56,188 --> 00:40:02,894
[SPEAKER_01]: You might think, ah, well, you know, it will end in death, destruction, and terror, and dread.

546
00:40:03,314 --> 00:40:04,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Stuff you fear throughout your life,

547
00:40:06,331 --> 00:40:08,392
[SPEAKER_01]: That direction of that fear, that's where death is.

548
00:40:09,352 --> 00:40:12,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sooner or later, you will be taken there.

549
00:40:13,293 --> 00:40:23,356
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might want to prepare yourself, or even if you're able to go through it beforehand, so when it arises, it doesn't concern you anymore, doesn't pertain to your being anymore.

550
00:40:23,396 --> 00:40:23,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

551
00:40:23,656 --> 00:40:24,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you extinguish it.

552
00:40:25,336 --> 00:40:25,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Gone.

553
00:40:26,116 --> 00:40:26,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Done.

554
00:40:27,517 --> 00:40:29,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Arjan Chah's quote, die before you die.

555
00:40:31,258 --> 00:40:31,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally.

556
00:40:33,975 --> 00:40:40,938
