729
00:56:29,597 --> 00:56:37,081
[SPEAKER_01]: But, basically, you saying yes or no should not make an emotional difference to anything you were about to do or you were doing.

730
00:56:37,821 --> 00:56:41,223
[SPEAKER_01]: If it is making difference, means that thing is emotionally evolving.

731
00:56:41,743 --> 00:56:44,405
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's emotionally evolving, means it's not wholesome.

732
00:56:45,606 --> 00:56:51,809
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, whatever you were about to do, just say no to yourself and see if any emotions are going to arise.

733
00:56:52,269 --> 00:56:57,692
[SPEAKER_01]: If they do, means you're involved there and your restraint, your saying no, is just revealing you that.

734
00:56:58,822 --> 00:57:01,344
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not adding anything to it.

735
00:57:01,364 --> 00:57:02,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Not taking away anything from it.

736
00:57:06,128 --> 00:57:10,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, that that pain of restraint is not actually pain of restraint.

737
00:57:10,831 --> 00:57:13,493
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pain of sensuality that's revealed through restraint.

738
00:57:15,995 --> 00:57:28,245
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see that sufficiently enough, some people sooner, some people later, you would, you would not, like, your mind wouldn't go back, wouldn't go to that sensuality whatsoever.

739
00:57:29,233 --> 00:57:31,534
[SPEAKER_01]: He could really relish and enjoy restraint.

740
00:57:34,074 --> 00:57:36,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Because restraint is like freedom from it.

741
00:57:39,256 --> 00:57:42,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and results in, well, concentration.

742
00:57:42,096 --> 00:57:45,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And, well, it results in escape from the whole.

743
00:57:47,418 --> 00:57:56,020
[SPEAKER_01]: But by not, by, sorry, but by attributing the pain to restraint, you're always going to have, like, a romantic

744
00:57:57,249 --> 00:58:00,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasurable, delightful notions of sensuality.

745
00:58:00,752 --> 00:58:08,439
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you're just grinding against through whatever sets of, like, morals and virtues that you adopted externally, but not really knowing why.

746
00:58:09,179 --> 00:58:11,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it always feels... And that's what we said.

747
00:58:11,701 --> 00:58:15,825
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't welcome, he doesn't delight the sensuality.

748
00:58:16,966 --> 00:58:18,507
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start practicing those attitudes.

749
00:58:18,527 --> 00:58:20,189
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't welcome it, you don't delight in it.

750
00:58:21,170 --> 00:58:24,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even if you practice the restraining, if you never ever engage

751
00:58:25,555 --> 00:58:32,160
[SPEAKER_01]: In any bodily act of sensuality, through any sense, you still can entertain it in your mind for the rest of your life.

752
00:58:34,402 --> 00:58:38,004
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you abandon it in your mind, then automatically you won't be able to engage in it by body.

753
00:58:39,165 --> 00:58:44,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's another simile in the suttas when, like, having a... trying to light a fire with two fire sticks that were in the water.

754
00:58:45,249 --> 00:58:47,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, okay, sure, you take them out of the water first.

755
00:58:47,791 --> 00:58:50,053
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't light them while they're still in the river.

756
00:58:51,154 --> 00:58:53,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But unless they dry outside of the water,

757
00:58:56,357 --> 00:58:58,018
[SPEAKER_01]: You still wouldn't be able to light a fire.

758
00:58:58,078 --> 00:59:00,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Even, okay, I'm out of the water, but it's still wet.

759
00:59:00,660 --> 00:59:04,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, one's mind still remains wet even when the body is withdrawn from sensuality.

760
00:59:05,483 --> 00:59:07,644
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind still remains wet in regard to it.

761
00:59:09,045 --> 00:59:12,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And the mind remains wet for as long as you're misplacing where the real pain comes from.

762
00:59:16,170 --> 00:59:19,552
[SPEAKER_01]: By misplacing the real pain, you look for the pleasure in the wrong place.

763
00:59:20,913 --> 00:59:22,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, consequently,

764
00:59:37,568 --> 00:59:55,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but it gets very confusing if you're trying to, you know, attack the mind, clear it out, adding this and adding that, fight it with kindness, destroy your... Well, you're just throwing more confusion onto the already existing pile of confusion.

765
00:59:56,797 --> 01:00:01,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you've got to make lists and make sure, oh, what do I do now?

766
01:00:01,699 --> 01:00:03,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Discounting, doing this, doing that.

767
01:00:03,419 --> 01:00:04,740
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's this, I do that.

768
01:00:05,140 --> 01:00:06,621
[SPEAKER_01]: But it all leaves you out.

769
01:00:07,322 --> 01:00:07,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

770
01:00:07,702 --> 01:00:17,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing this in regard to that, but none of it really addresses you nor the very criteria of what made you choose to decide to do this against that in the first place.

771
01:00:17,708 --> 01:00:19,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's already affected by ignorance.

772
01:00:20,050 --> 01:00:25,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you took it for granted that your power of decision is already right and knows the way out.

773
01:00:25,713 --> 01:00:27,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll just do this when I do that.

774
01:00:27,294 --> 01:00:27,934
[SPEAKER_01]: This makes sense.

775
01:00:28,795 --> 01:00:35,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, if somebody's honest and they know that, okay, I don't have a right view, thus, even what makes sense to me is not right.

776
01:00:36,232 --> 01:00:36,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Necessarily.

777
01:00:36,973 --> 01:00:37,613
[SPEAKER_01]: I do not know.

778
01:00:40,756 --> 01:00:42,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you really have to stop.

779
01:00:43,858 --> 01:00:50,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, certainly, you certainly have to stop using the criteria of what feels right and what feels good.

780
01:00:51,904 --> 01:00:54,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, it's because of your feelings that you are not enlightened.

781
01:00:56,128 --> 01:01:00,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, using that as a criteria towards enlightenment is a contradiction in terms.

782
01:01:00,651 --> 01:01:02,633
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to use discernment.

783
01:01:03,553 --> 01:01:05,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, start developing discernment.

784
01:01:06,134 --> 01:01:07,055
[SPEAKER_01]: What makes sense?

785
01:01:07,775 --> 01:01:08,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Use your reason.

786
01:01:08,436 --> 01:01:10,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Use... develop wisdom, understanding.

787
01:01:11,558 --> 01:01:15,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, bit by bit, on top of that, you add a bit more, you build more, and it will progress.

788
01:01:17,082 --> 01:01:24,908
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then result in discernment of the fundamental things, such as your feelings, your intentions, and so on, and then free you from it.

789
01:01:30,473 --> 01:01:34,056
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, all the good I ever talked about, really, fundamentally, is understanding.

790
01:01:34,576 --> 01:01:35,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing understanding.

791
01:01:37,038 --> 01:01:39,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing things clearly, he knows, he sees.

792
01:01:40,280 --> 01:01:42,681
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows that he knows, sees and he sees.

